    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2002-p4.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Take \\(n=1.\\) Then \\(m\\cdot 1\\le m+1\\) becomes \\(m\\le m+1,\\) which holds for every positive integer \\(m.\\)\n\nSo every positive integer \\(m\\) works, giving infinitely many.\n\nThus, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Try a very small positive value of \\(n\\)",
        "See whether the resulting inequality places any upper bound on \\(m\\)"
      ],
